<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wismar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wismar is a Baltic port town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> received Wismar along with the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. These acquisitions gave Sweden a foothold in Germany and votes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wismar.jpg" alt="Wismar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Assenmacher · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wismar_Georgenkirche_Blick_auf_Nikolaikirche.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wismar lies on the Bay of Wismar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, opposite the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poel">Poel</a>, midway between the port cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> to the west and Rostock to the east. The state capital Schwerin lies to the south on Lake Schwerin. The city has a natural harbour protected by a promontory. It is the fourth-largest city of Mecklenburg and now the capital of the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordwestmecklenburg">Nordwestmecklenburg</a>. Its name was first recorded in the 12th century and is probably of Slavic origin, though this is disputed, and the area belonged to the territory of the western Slavic Obotrites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The date of the city's foundation is not clear. It is estimated at 1226 under Henry Borwin I, Lord of Mecklenburg, and the oldest document of 1229 already shows civic rights. In 1259 Wismar joined a defensive agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and Rostock against Baltic pirates, which formed a basis for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. By the 13th and 14th centuries it was a flourishing trading hub and a centre of wool processing. About 2,000 inhabitants died in the plague of 1376, but the town remained reasonably prosperous until the 16th century.</p><p>After 1648 Wismar was Swedish, which entitled the Swedish kings, as imperial princes, to a seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. After 1653 the Fürstenhof was the seat of the supreme court for all Swedish dominions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and Field Marshal Erik Dahlbergh extended the fortifications. Danish forces captured the town in 1675 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scanian-war">Scanian War</a>.</p><p>In 1803 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> ceded Wismar to the Grand Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin for 1,258,000 Riksdalers, reserving a right of redemption, and renounced its claims in 1903. In the Second World War Wismar was heavily bombed; British paratroopers took it on 2 May 1945, and it passed to the Soviet occupation zone on 1 July 1945. It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>'s second-largest port.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Wismar's old town, with Brick Gothic buildings of the 13th to 15th centuries and a large market square, was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2002 together with the old town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a>. The square holds the Wasserkunst, a wrought-iron fountain imported from Holland in 1602, and a house called the Alter Schwede, built about 1380.</p><p>The tower of St. Mary's is all that remains of the original church. The city's Swedish period is marked each year by a Sweden Celebration. Shipbuilding, begun at the site in 1946, remains important, and the city has a university of applied sciences, the Hochschule Wismar. The 1922 film Nosferatu was partly shot there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wismar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They formed a sworn association on Gotland, trading through Visby to Novgorod, and new German towns such as Riga, Reval (Tallinn), Danzig (Gdańsk), Rostock, <b>Wismar</b> and Stralsund were founded along the southern and eastern coasts, many of them under Lübeck law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the seventeenth century Swedish intervention rescued the Protestant cause in the Thirty Years’ War, and the Peace of Westphalia of 1648 gave Sweden Western Pomerania, Bremen-Verden and <b>Wismar</b> and a voice in the Holy Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Altmark (1629), the mouth of the Oder River in Pomerania in the early 1630s, and the territories formalised in the Peace of Westphalia (1648), which gave Sweden Western Pomerania, the bishoprics of Bremen and Verden and the port of <b>Wismar</b>, together with a voice in the Imperial Diet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick William received the larger western share as the Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin, with Schwerin, Rostock and <b>Wismar's</b> hinterland, while a smaller share became Mecklenburg-Strelitz.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-schwerin">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a></li><li><blockquote>Sweden occupied <b>Wismar</b> during the Thirty Years' War, and the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 gave it the town, the island of Poel and the district of Neukloster as an imperial fief.</blockquote><a href="/state/swedish-wismar">Swedish Wismar</a></li></ul></section>
